Runtime media-type catalog
==========================

This directory maintains runtime MIME identities independently of the frozen
legacy enum inputs in misc/mime.types and misc/mime_cad.types. Builds use the
checked-in data and require no network access or desktop MIME installation.

Files
-----

upstream.types       Deterministic projection of a pinned mime-db snapshot.
upstream.json        Upstream commit, URL, SHA-256 and license.
mime-db.LICENSE      License text for that snapshot.
supplement.json      Reviewed identities, extensions, aliases and provenance.
update.py            Reproducible upstream snapshot updater.
desktop.cmake        Private desktop-database association checks.

src/libbu/mime_catalog.cmake combines the upstream projection and supplement
into runtime tables. Legacy enum translation is generated separately by
src/libbu/mime.cmake. Keep the old input rows and order unchanged: their enum
integers remain compatibility values during the deprecation window.

Naming policy
-------------

1. Prefer the IANA media-types registry, including its top-level category.
   For example, DXF is image/vnd.dxf.
2. For an unregistered format, prefer an established name published by its
   owner or desktop metadata. FreeCAD publishes application/x-extension-fcstd
   and OpenSCAD publishes application/x-openscad. Compatibility with those
   existing names is not a policy of creating new x- names.
3. Fill genuine gaps with convention-compliant vendor- or personal-tree
   proposals following RFC 6838. Mark them proposed. They are neither IANA
   registrations nor endorsements by the named projects. Registration needs
   the owner's involvement, a stable specification and a completed template.

Do not manufacture names from SDK driver identifiers. A backend is an
implementation, not a data format. A structured suffix identifies a
registered underlying syntax, such as +xml; do not invent +asc or +binary.
OGC discussion paper 12-029's x-ogc proposals are scoped to web services and
are not a general desktop naming scheme.

Keep distinct representations separate. A label, its raster data and an
archive containing both may require different identities. A shared extension
is an association, not an alias: .vtu, for example, can refer to registered
Virtue3D model/vnd.vtu or proposed application/vnd.kitware.vtk+xml.

Supplement records
------------------

Each type records its name, status, extensions and primary references.
Statuses distinguish registered names, established unregistered names and
local proposals. Notes explain identity boundaries or non-obvious choices;
format revisions and decoder support belong in traits and capabilities.
registration_candidate is informational and creates no identity or alias.

Use references from IANA, the format owner, its specification or published
desktop metadata. Preserve the distinction between owner-provided desktop
XML and the central shared-mime-info database. An SDK's extension list alone
does not establish a MIME identity or conversion support.

Aliases must name the same format family. They do not assert a revision,
encoding or other profile. Retain published external names when needed;
incorrect internal labels translate only at the legacy enum boundary. The
supplement's legacy map can translate an old label or retire it with null.
Do not publish every obsolete internal spelling as a runtime alias.

BRL-CAD conventions
------------------

Native V4 and V5 databases use proposed model/vnd.brlcad, distinguished by a
version trait. The published packaging labels application/brlcad-v4,
application/brlcad-v5 and application/brlcad-unknown remain aliases.
ASC uses proposed application/vnd.brlcad.script for its separate grammar.

Debian desktop entries, MIME XML and icon aliases must agree on the canonical
identity. Content rules recognize a full 128-byte V4 identification record
and the eight-byte V5 header, including empty V5 databases. Filename globs
alone do not distinguish native databases from unrelated .g files.

Updating upstream data
----------------------

Pin a full upstream commit rather than a moving branch:

    python3 misc/mime/update.py --commit <full-commit>

For an offline update or verification, supply the matching upstream files:

    python3 misc/mime/update.py --commit <full-commit> \
        --source /path/to/db.json --license /path/to/LICENSE
    python3 misc/mime/update.py --check \
        --source /path/to/db.json --license /path/to/LICENSE

Review upstream.types, upstream.json and mime-db.LICENSE together. Review
extension collisions and supplement overrides; do not copy the snapshot
back into the frozen enum inputs. The updater does not edit local proposals
or decide that two names are aliases.

Adding or changing an identity
-----------------------------

* Check authoritative sources and existing supplement records first.
* Record status and provenance, with notes only where the boundary needs
  explanation. Mark proposed names accurately.
* Preserve extension collisions and review every alias explicitly.
* Add recognition separately from decoder capabilities. Advertise only
  variants and operations implemented by the configured handler.
* Check canonicalization, aliases, detection and affected conversions.
  Run bu_mime_registry and bu_mime_desktop when applicable, plus the
  regress-repository and regress-licenses targets.

The runtime API, trait contracts and plugin guidance are documented in
  doc/asciidoc/articles/mime.adoc
The deprecation inventory and removal gates are in
  doc/changes/deprecated.adoc

References
----------

IANA registry: https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-types/media-types.xhtml
Naming rules: https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6838
Structured suffixes: https://www.iana.org/assignments/media-type-structured-suffix/media-type-structured-suffix.xhtml
Desktop metadata: https://specifications.freedesktop.org/shared-mime-info-spec/latest/
Upstream data: https://github.com/jshttp/mime-db
Per-format primary sources are recorded in supplement.json.
